<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Chi Tu</h1><p class="st-meta">550 CE – 650 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The 'Red Earth Kingdom' of Chinese sources, a Buddhist state on the Malay Peninsula visited by a Sui embassy in 607.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Chi Tu is known almost entirely from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> shu, which describes a mission sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang of Sui</a> in 607 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chang-jun">Chang Jun</a>. The account calls it a branch of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/funan">Funan</a> and places it on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a>, probably in the region of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kelantan-sultanate">Kelantan</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pattani-kingdom">Pattani</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songkhla">Songkhla</a>, but its location has never been confirmed.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Chinese envoys described a king of the Qutan (Gautama) family who lived in a palace with three-storeyed gates, and a court where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> rites were practised. They were received with ceremony, gifts of food served on leaves and a gold-leaf letter. The kingdom traded local products along the peninsular coast.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chang-jun">Chang Jun's</a> visit and a return mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 610, Chi Tu vanishes from the record. It was probably absorbed into the expanding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> network or neighbouring peninsular states such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/langkasuka">Langkasuka</a>. Its name survives mainly as an example of early Chinese ethnography of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/chi-tu.jpg" alt="Chi Tu" width="320" height="385"><figcaption>Image: L joo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ChiTu005.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>550 CE – 650 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Hinduism</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>, <a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Red Earth Kingdom, Chitu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=600&amp;focus=chi-tu" aria-label="Chi Tu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7439 1603 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chi Tu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7769 1191h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7759 1859h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7759 1859h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Thailand. <a href="/atlas?year=600&amp;focus=chi-tu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

